#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125288} :activity/id #uuid "2f9b26dc-7c78-5bb7-9eb0-e6029679e710" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a0c271d5-ca3c-5651-a78d-a2b18242c45c" :step/math-academy-id 11087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436129}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "9e271cad-793b-51f0-9124-47a943c42f3e" :step/math-academy-id 11090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436128}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "98229d4f-e216-5858-8b87-e9442dce8ede" :step/math-academy-id 11092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125732 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a9b09430-2391-566d-ac41-6360e135665e" :step/math-academy-id 11094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508069}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "b6bdeefa-e28b-5e72-8c05-e9f8fb9c7542" :step/math-academy-id 11197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "6285e0db-558c-5e11-85ab-0b82dc834963" :step/math-academy-id 15456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050225}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534002}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "af7ce556-917b-54ac-a772-adecff1e36f2" :step/math-academy-id 23425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"} :step/id #uuid "29991074-71d2-5a37-a971-5f658f37d3ba" :step/math-academy-id 23498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297}}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124724}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852} :activity/id #uuid "a4f417dd-5f5b-58a7-ab74-5a973fc151bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8958422f-4c02-53af-a3e5-28f8445d5bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377710} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7dc054-8a42-5213-b120-534bafd7ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9cc3c-2948-54af-96ab-97ac3dc5dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377712} {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2466e9-2081-57a2-b9cc-0260610356d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-74965" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23171e8d-210d-5c2c-9bfd-bb23fda5150e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1408b7a6-0c53-5b8e-9133-67f17a92be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "195220e5-a0bf-53a4-9715-d520b9cc06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9919cf-e851-589f-8631-eb084d75d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832} {:answer/id #uuid "af13b1b6-a010-5d4f-9f5a-9200139b2880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} :answer-field/id #uuid "356ed38c-7b44-5965-81ac-2c88dd16e0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-74955" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"} :step/id #uuid "61b825c0-1272-5e4c-b3ff-4f0f75ec0886" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c4d527-5e01-5264-8001-c16ba5c829f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453880} {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d46ebdc-4ea8-5b5d-a791-12ebf82b2b2b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} {:answer/id #uuid "9233290a-d139-5d2d-91b5-3115178cf2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9a1015-1583-5e39-b729-2685f362e667" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} {:answer/id #uuid "6013b3d6-d8f8-5f12-8351-83ccb30ce208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} :answer-field/id #uuid "81040326-5134-5a42-8539-aac88ecd9a5e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- **III.** The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80843e02-eff6-534a-9c80-13ab1c07a9fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8e3137-e924-50eb-b2e3-83c1445d70ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e8e33d31-3246-51ca-a270-794ee881c78b"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407821}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475988} :activity/id #uuid "8ffeb28b-7042-5986-93be-16c63236c88f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d118fc5-1c52-5566-be98-a9707e774937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377697} {:answer/id #uuid "30aa323e-111a-55db-ae16-f96b2c70da3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} {:answer/id #uuid "563f0bf5-f3be-54dc-b44e-fce9a03892d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} {:answer/id #uuid "a81b9d36-0a30-5151-bf36-ec51a5b12490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377700} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b3786-3b86-57a7-acce-0bc703bbfb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} :answer-field/id #uuid "117ca5de-bfde-5504-8e9b-162dc1ee9296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d22711b-d1d6-5f06-ab4a-74275f71d77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74920" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "13485a67-00ba-5d4e-a4ce-60195066ea29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a374dad-4f6b-524a-aa54-86b138bf78f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342993} {:answer/id #uuid "63a029bd-31fb-55ab-850e-9758cb89df9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343081} {:answer/id #uuid "8849f804-0c8c-53f5-8a8d-500546d52a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343419} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9051dc-e91b-5b6b-8909-a2310cbbc28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343442} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e25dd-2c46-5b76-99a3-260d2e1e558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343081} :answer-field/id #uuid "b313de74-d195-5d45-bc88-f2346a3b2f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "774103b5-b3b5-52ef-9d71-0bba2999450c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74957" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3925855e-b8a5-539c-a189-1799c0ceb8ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a6788-72f7-50c1-a035-0612abea7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380244} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6b773-681c-58f9-b0ee-b6ee7442117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380245} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a1ac-d347-5b5f-9ae4-3572ba829da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380246} {:answer/id #uuid "91b1447d-4781-51f6-b677-110d8689bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} {:answer/id #uuid "8852c79a-f54e-5a8f-87c0-2803442d73d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f7b683-d099-548b-9b98-dddfeba7f07a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb25e06c-c7b0-5dbe-83ae-8f7b0c5dbf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-86868" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c7e6dac-de5a-50c9-a4a3-207c8cf4283a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5225a54e-55a7-5758-ada6-d6e76ec121f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342920} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe5058-ed29-5490-84ca-cf5ea037f221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343150} {:answer/id #uuid "72810408-6a46-5e8d-9beb-52bbe2e8f436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343231} {:answer/id #uuid "c186d6e7-0ac4-5259-897c-07e60f6cc561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343939} {:answer/id #uuid "fd70e6c7-52f3-50c1-9a16-a6b4b41da448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343150} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5121edc-c04d-5ea2-9e7a-1a1ca983765e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "580ed3de-301a-5585-aa08-af0b5b637cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-74960" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "a1d76783-98ca-587b-9e64-3ae5ae7e97c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1771b53b-a9ea-5204-9e6c-c3664e7f4eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} {:answer/id #uuid "52b82999-7d0c-5f60-b09d-5d9bfb9dabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475563} {:answer/id #uuid "644db317-5ce2-5b55-a3a9-b57771242cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475590} {:answer/id #uuid "9e112b0b-f642-5793-ba13-d921da5ca298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475677} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d267a1-bd0b-57f4-92ed-545ceb83bbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe5df83-a1f1-5212-94d6-8a03a2936996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83c8f2ad-db17-53f8-868d-e48deabc8feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129409" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=2$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=x+1$\n\nIII. The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{(1-1)(2-2)}{(1-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0(0)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(1,2)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=2}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(2-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(0)}{(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=x+1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x+1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x-1)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}}{2\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d56691a3-bd81-5d30-bd36-57d7d11c4bf9"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475440}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508127} :activity/id #uuid "ceebe888-3e8e-5cd4-adb8-cd12e96beae4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc3fc2e-8291-595d-9efe-37b54c54ec70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507628} {:answer/id #uuid "21b545b6-d2ce-5675-b1f3-a77567c762a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507636} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507789} {:answer/id #uuid "8f99aa38-d301-56e1-857e-d573e65c98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507801} {:answer/id #uuid "acdc3a68-84af-5610-9099-7c787b10a4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507789} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b72334-231e-5d57-bdc0-d2fb9585f9c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74956" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1) & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "3074aaf8-a64d-58be-886f-5f8b626d0bc8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b2e738-d4ff-5a73-bf88-ee96a546601e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263713} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5d18cd-26e9-513a-9ac2-d9e28cdc3a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263957} {:answer/id #uuid "859fba6d-65df-52f9-8934-78218673bfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264151} {:answer/id #uuid "b3999021-6e0d-58f6-8af2-534ecd301f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264540} {:answer/id #uuid "f16bc0b6-a366-5c5f-a505-fcaf77e8d63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265120} :answer-field/id #uuid "828835a1-d776-5ae6-8933-2aafe083fed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8bc5519-0c3a-5438-9140-d93c11fd7b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-129342" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\cancel{x-y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"} :step/id #uuid "33d52943-cf7c-5ddf-bb1f-113936cfa6d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508167}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154cde04-acab-5465-9547-10829d1c2ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342426} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb42284-ebac-5722-a55a-f3d61f6c5806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343155} {:answer/id #uuid "92dfc28e-190d-569d-937c-6a93104fd226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343523} {:answer/id #uuid "b4342938-2831-524c-937b-68dbdb102e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343816} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa824e-6288-5d0e-ba41-9737c9cd645c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342426} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e203f08-9966-5d66-bfae-16443836045d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "267b24cc-56d4-5780-90e2-1ea13cc5ba4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74918" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "3c399357-4d7f-55ba-a5f1-d7571818aae3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7b2bc548-78f2-5e4e-b985-befdf65ed49c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c9e982-2244-5a23-aac9-ea8f242dfd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507626} {:answer/id #uuid "2c45373f-5f36-5270-9c27-ce7558fa56c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507653} {:answer/id #uuid "59f30dc1-5f5d-5fdc-a739-06dad6b23c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507728} {:answer/id #uuid "5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507731} {:answer/id #uuid "824d43de-2c6c-5256-ab7d-e1e81ca546b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507731} :answer-field/id #uuid "f962e375-138e-5ba3-a11f-61cf507ac9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129401" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(1,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,x=1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ee66d94f-00d8-5e5e-84ff-35a7175f7dff"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507605}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199091} :activity/id #uuid "8bc28183-1c22-5cc0-8df5-bd16b54f088d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048592 :topic/id #uuid "ac50c7f3-cf1f-565d-96af-32bd8ce3b7b9" :topic/math-academy-id 4254} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199541 :tutorial/content "Recall that a *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some universal set and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either true or false.\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ x \\in U \\: | \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $D(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $2,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $U = \\big\\{ 1,2, \\ldots, 6 \\big\\}.$\n\nTo find the truth set $T,$ we need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nLet's check all the elements in our universal set $U$ to determine which of them are elements of the truth set $T{:}$\n\n- $x=1\\notin T$ since $1$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=2\\in T$ since $2$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=3\\notin T$ since $3$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=4\\in T$ since $4$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=5\\notin T$ since $5$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=6\\in T$ since $6$ is divisible by $2$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ 2,4,6 \\big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8422ca20-ab2c-5e41-a789-3c21465dfc51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8139 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbb18ea5-8c55-58c8-81e5-4c923006baa2" :step/math-academy-id 153521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198096 :question/id #uuid "4b253527-f5ca-5927-97f0-d91491d598b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c23caadd-1738-549f-aade-2c983dc250dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050073}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425978}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Predicate"} :step/id #uuid "3cbfca6e-271d-50e8-8a2d-2854b810036b" :step/math-academy-id 153522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199540 :tutorial/content "A predicate of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains these variables with the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false. \n\nWe can denote such a predicate using the following notation:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nIf $U_1$ is the universal set for $x_1,U_2$ is the universal set for $x_2,\\ldots,$ etc, then the universal set $U$ for our predicate is given by the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nU = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n.\n$$\n\nFor example, the sentence\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x,y): x+y \\lt 2,$ $\\quad$ $x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nis a predicate of two variables, $x$ and $y.$ In this case, the universal set is\n\n$$\nU = \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} = \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples $(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n) \\in U$ such that substituting these tuples into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: | \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, to determine the truth set of the predicate $P(x,y)$ defined above, we need to find $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x+y \\lt 2$\n\nTo find the truth set, we first find an explicit condition on $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & <2 \\\\ y & <2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set contains all tuples $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ that satisfy this inequality. We can write the truth set $T$ of our predicate as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: y < 2-x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $T$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we can represent it as a region in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebd1253b4980e2be41247128a426412adafe34a64fdc38d2d7141cb20d62354.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cec17b5-8507-56d1-8683-8c2a82af211d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8138 :tutorial/title "Predicates With More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "aef558ff-96f1-5037-bf03-b3799da39d24" :step/math-academy-id 153525 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :question/id #uuid "14dc17f1-056e-5e82-81e6-c3964b7df2d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534037}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "fc98c6d5-6d1d-5d6a-9c44-fa2bf85d1072" :step/math-academy-id 153526 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198098 :question/id #uuid "3d212cb2-394b-5f9d-8e7d-8406ec7ca580" :question/math-academy-id "e-12723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9703bd-92ae-51f7-902b-251a7947ba6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051063}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481668}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Multivariable Predicate"} :step/id #uuid "99a08b9b-5aec-5987-b707-883afcf5ea45" :step/math-academy-id 153529}] :activity/title "Truth Sets of Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198513}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120699} :activity/id #uuid "59d43d6d-e407-5159-a37a-73328284da1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048762 :topic/id #uuid "bc92d9a7-3663-5495-a1b9-4cf735034158" :topic/math-academy-id 1865} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121043 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the vector\n\n![](images/2d/2dabd318dc49628f9333642f06492d3303617a0ea1cade93c0470c7a767121f5.svg)\n\nNow, let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be another basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = \\mathbf{c}_2 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}?$\n\nSince ![](images/3e/3e330c07260a16bc76853ca1d275bbd9759f60179dce22500ee6dd38f326ca20.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-({c}_{2})-(-2{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/5d/5d39ae32e2bad5125e6d7cf8ba7b943f2c926e1f1d6525ce019fd9a40c263bce.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d5021c80-ecd5-57d1-b176-3ac06c0c4b38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbbd8b68-9c01-5dc1-a46f-54a0bc690766" :step/math-academy-id 17517 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119634 :question/id #uuid "6a082146-0d63-59dd-9224-a319d73ca59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4827"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432501}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"} :step/id #uuid "4b2867fd-9b0b-58c0-86fd-8aee91a58c36" :step/math-academy-id 17519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119635 :question/id #uuid "f07356b8-aeb3-50ac-b017-c370d87395d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437063}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b2e04f32-eaf4-5a63-8709-0ac4376c51b7" :step/math-academy-id 17520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119636 :question/id #uuid "91fac4cd-7097-5244-a385-9f3f76395912" :question/math-academy-id "e-4829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437064}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"} :step/id #uuid "344d2e0b-3884-5f31-a733-c6d1b647154b" :step/math-academy-id 17521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121070 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/be/bec3577dd261660f2545adb66b9c26fbb520ea60aa6b0337094347dbc1937ff4.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{b}_1 = 2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2?$\n\nSince ![](images/20/200509c629fdf3d263ff375722f57ed77012a8a91a58a7e7f507fad3765b3782.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-2({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =10{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\  & =8{c}_{1}-7{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/6b/6bc09e66faaef40bba491f31c9342d4d08a8fb6eb3fd4dd1a3e2acdd77421741.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "189050f1-7dc3-53be-a9f6-52abe95d9915" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4287 :tutorial/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"} :step/id #uuid "a41a07f8-567e-599b-ae5d-a130ad3aae2c" :step/math-academy-id 26017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120702}}] :activity/title "Writing Vectors in Different Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120055}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424440} :activity/id #uuid "f5cbb860-2adf-582c-a552-bdba6343726b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048762 :topic/id #uuid "bc92d9a7-3663-5495-a1b9-4cf735034158" :topic/math-academy-id 1865} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eba5ccd-5c4e-5a36-a064-4ef635ea962c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380638} {:answer/id #uuid "153e965c-0ba4-51b3-b2cc-0690963e8754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380639} {:answer/id #uuid "26addb96-9bcf-50c8-96e8-46e354758c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380640} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6c74e4-e674-54ce-a79c-25df9fdacc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380641} {:answer/id #uuid "97952375-bc23-52ad-9a79-64c3a8443c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380639} :answer-field/id #uuid "76499bf7-e986-58f6-9967-cfbce16c554a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c19afb3-436b-5bc9-8f6f-99c189134e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112812" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=3{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2}+8{b}_{3} \\\\  & =8{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(4{b}_{3})+2(2{b}_{1})-2(3{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7799965b-8388-5cb7-a5d4-d8203068d7c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424441}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8abef8-6bb8-54af-9cab-7f8200ea2b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348983} {:answer/id #uuid "7d38cee2-216a-50f7-9ee2-2046c8fd2070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349595} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5e25ed-9a4e-51e9-91a9-88573309ce14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349614} {:answer/id #uuid "da13cf86-203f-5c39-afea-626810d1dd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350359} {:answer/id #uuid "decdfd78-92ff-5e5e-bfcb-c0b319ddfdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{29}{2} \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348983} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9346877-f860-5d75-8662-7c418ed4209e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c9bca47-36fc-5691-b4a4-81a309a1be29" :question/math-academy-id "q-80826" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "97cd0ede-149c-58ac-816c-c5ea02b44304" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1521a735-440e-5594-83fc-552a8de81b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348626} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8320a6-b66a-50c6-966f-ec2b07922217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349430} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8ac099-04dd-5736-8947-f97096f61cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349469} {:answer/id #uuid "ee795864-027f-5202-bbf2-2a8d67b17d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350530} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1f1b8a-56b4-5f6d-896b-8508eb1fe82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b9dd274-e82b-583b-8183-dfabcc8cb925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5391d763-accb-53ad-9d75-896f6a1f5e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-80814" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}={b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+6{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+6{b}_{3}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3(-{b}_{2})+3(2{b}_{3})+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a9fec6b0-dfaa-5c4f-81aa-7c81022a0299" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a1b0ee-a4a2-5576-8c66-7aaec2ee0e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424424} {:answer/id #uuid "22b18ee1-002f-5c66-b260-413039c7fa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424426} {:answer/id #uuid "44ad6232-afa5-5eee-acd4-ac7923c6c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424427} {:answer/id #uuid "54f9c040-8ec5-5672-a995-f18c78ec39cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424429} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d735a-e23a-5d22-b726-fc09edb321eb" :answ