" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=-6{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =15{b}_{1}-24{b}_{2}+18{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-24{b}_{2}+18{b}_{3}+15{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-8(3{b}_{2})-3(-6{b}_{3})+5(3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-8{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}+5{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0e41dcfd-ebac-5aca-b944-04ffca0796dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a201169-6127-5146-ac33-370dbaeb9fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437052} {:answer/id #uuid "6d16febb-185e-5f02-b767-d0d169b5dbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437053} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf45f1d-e7fe-5ad3-af94-a089a9eeec5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437054} {:answer/id #uuid "e664d5e6-21e0-5873-967f-9bc8ff95c930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437057} {:answer/id #uuid "eb796ee2-fa6f-5226-9fdb-c601791894cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437060}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437052} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfb7050-a4fe-5646-9ebf-d0c4cc689423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "52843c58-6ada-5ce6-8fe3-a0a1c897ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80817" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=5{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=-3{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =3{b}_{3}+8{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-4{b}_{3})+\\frac{8}{5}(5{b}_{1})+\\frac{2}{3}(-3{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}{c}_{1}+\\frac{8}{5}{c}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "59637308-9cf1-5b00-baa5-bb0d666676d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4d1efa-0b11-5604-b83d-1cafc0e12e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437051} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b65cef-3b11-5aa5-8c39-6a1bd856ef09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437055} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6a6cb4-14b9-5dc8-a71d-66abf40eedf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437056} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f678ca-51ca-5faa-80f3-6687002731fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437058} {:answer/id #uuid "e97d3d12-325a-5425-988a-d90f7923ca9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437059} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a1919-efd3-511f-9ea6-f55df071b113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0e4cc3b-3144-5373-932e-12723fa2aecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-112862" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-5(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{2}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "97825df6-b0ae-5545-b8e4-947e68d7f416" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1f3a69-7740-5aa2-925c-53c1ce3286b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319532} {:answer/id #uuid "66b1a873-459f-552e-a7e5-027c42c7521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319705} {:answer/id #uuid "8891e75e-27cb-5964-9e25-018084c2ca28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319799} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ae0bb-3fdb-536c-87d0-6e5d6c437047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319862} {:answer/id #uuid "c86c86a5-b326-5d1c-b7a9-87d860ce9998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319862} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da19070-3706-599c-9a4c-66595b84535c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8522320-65f1-531f-bee8-8f3ee0c26cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35257" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-6{b}_{2}+3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}(5{b}_{2})-\\frac{3}{2}(-2{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}{c}_{1}-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c00bf068-2766-50a6-930e-60ed75e6af41" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06929580-b4ba-53af-ac69-8bc84360d71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432469} {:answer/id #uuid "6599960a-b761-54ae-800a-4099e8dc6454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432480} {:answer/id #uuid "7238f33a-029e-5920-a373-cb968bcc10aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432481} {:answer/id #uuid "7960b759-a049-517f-91ce-8f3d3d4ab81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432482} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5905dd-9d56-563e-ab3a-014628903d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432480} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c066721-df4d-58dc-9748-1ef1119a9c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f3bd7e4-e9ee-5ec1-b495-697d442c712e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113070" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(-{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-(-{c}_{2})+3({c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "edd9f346-183f-5c36-8e34-bd14bd1d6d98"}] :activity/title "Writing Vectors in Different Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437050}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478491} :activity/id #uuid "b8cd0170-12ba-5a13-8bd5-54a9ef34a08b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048762 :topic/id #uuid "bc92d9a7-3663-5495-a1b9-4cf735034158" :topic/math-academy-id 1865} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20bb3464-f73d-5921-8cdf-f112ab14ead9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410019} {:answer/id #uuid "36299c18-1f12-5691-a4cc-cf600875d435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410022} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7a28ee-dddd-5aff-9301-8500542e8d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410031} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ea2e8b-285d-5d4a-86b6-3118e3eacc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410041} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b40102-f333-5f07-9467-4d1fb5ba690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410019} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e25391-7c4e-5c7c-8ae1-5b47ead04007" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f46f0e34-3d5c-5e7a-9fb4-690993802dbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-81230" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. By adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "06723a52-a509-54e8-a958-1ee3d11c3842" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b734f7-2e56-5969-b91b-fd5d7834144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244533} {:answer/id #uuid "55d5c155-aa97-5099-976e-267010b9f696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245069} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbe273e-6df1-5251-8f0b-69494a5c2141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245440} {:answer/id #uuid "8a957706-ca6f-56cc-883e-020725d0d396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245527} {:answer/id #uuid "b92afccc-c74b-5377-acc9-db0f72855208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245967} :answer-field/id #uuid "e597727d-4abc-5787-967c-bd4759284ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9c8b3d18-f99b-57ac-806c-9402fb0d526b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112855" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the equations, we get  \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2})-3(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{1}-12{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "374bc840-79bd-5b15-a6b4-c10eeb6fda83"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acab282-04ec-5eb0-ae3a-7ab839526ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410024} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9fccc8-89c8-5084-95ed-898b492b9317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410037} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b103c-e7ac-56f6-8025-6eee882f5637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410038} {:answer/id #uuid "f26fb59e-92cb-5f2d-bb4b-31538da03416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410047} {:answer/id #uuid "f9547433-2214-505b-b38c-379846b86b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410049} :answer-field/id #uuid "66650074-ff68-5eca-a6ce-c53f9965f0b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c92100bd-871b-5993-bed2-e255a4c7bcbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35246" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7fbd79a1-9017-5962-8464-c8335e25c404" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43596b54-0af5-56a0-a954-2be5f40712fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244900} {:answer/id #uuid "8b479d14-ade5-5b29-9efe-2745148ba46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245536} {:answer/id #uuid "be780025-c235-58a6-8299-86b26eaeee98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246012} {:answer/id #uuid "e089e494-fe55-562d-9268-21868311ca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246310} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c12ab0-ecc5-5590-8676-6569049bf30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245536} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d089c3-601c-50be-8dc4-891bcf11d265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5c48c40-2fd0-5515-867f-1f3be83f3dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-112656" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}+8{b}_{3} \\\\  & =8{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(4{b}_{3})+2{b}_{1}+2\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+4{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9647de70-ff9d-5f4b-bc4e-14a190bb842f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252cd9d2-cd28-51d0-86dc-f100bfbf8425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410020} {:answer/id #uuid "65ce31d1-a577-5b44-a7fa-3c3f399d7aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410028} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f4cd2-57fd-5205-92b4-a5bf14cc8ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 6 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410040} {:answer/id #uuid "e15510ae-a912-51f2-b92f-d981d02b25f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410045} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8e8b08-4548-5fe8-adbd-3bce3207c85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410028} :answer-field/id #uuid "de484491-607f-5712-bf20-9fe92c23a765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59ed1787-672a-57bb-b86d-43224b917d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34772" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=4{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3{b}_{1}+4{b}_{2}-6{b}_{3} \\\\  & =4{b}_{2}-6{b}_{3}-3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(3{b}_{2})+6(-{b}_{3})-\\frac{3}{4}(4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}-\\frac{3}{4}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6f946eb-8d53-5f69-9732-0e531c296bb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478481}}] :activity/title "Writing Vectors in Different Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477731}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152970} :activity/id #uuid "50b01990-2284-52a1-b046-89a46d877bf3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048864 :topic/id #uuid "c788c893-5c00-5b28-be4c-bd65dc3ed101" :topic/math-academy-id 2789} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153433 :tutorial/content "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty:\n\n$$\nC\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92860310-1be6-50cf-8447-2b24a95db5e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4c6c5e7f-d5d8-5038-8ce3-1957ac96ecd0" :step/math-academy-id 157019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151817 :question/id #uuid "841e5dcb-c092-5159-bfc4-d6580999c742" :question/math-academy-id "e-12760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534020}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"} :step/id #uuid "0ba98889-a41c-532a-bc68-6e4b156abe4a" :step/math-academy-id 157020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153434 :tutorial/content "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union. \n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since \n\n$$\n\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,\n$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{0,2,4\\}⊔\\{1,3\\} & =\\{0,2,4\\}\\cup \\{1,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d264ae6-080d-5ca0-be06-b6d41a5f4aa7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8005 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "a0fe9f92-abdf-5afd-94bd-7e0416495b3f" :step/math-academy-id 157023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152102 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151818 :question/id #uuid "ea87c6f2-d0ef-5f8d-8f87-b9219272cedd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7079378e-2cec-550d-a647-405a0acb9b61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425787}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "7342dbf9-8375-507b-9fcc-03ddc4dd27a7" :step/math-academy-id 157024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153436 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the following overlapping sets: \n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.\n$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. \n\n$$\nA_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTo construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{◻,△\\}⊔\\{♡,◻\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1)\\}\\cup \\{(♡,2),(◻,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e3b30b0-95ac-5b38-a7e6-169cc3409776" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8137 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "233824ca-a0f9-5e23-af03-ba0c108fc90c" :step/math-academy-id 157027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151819 :question/id #uuid "65852684-d9ce-5830-b95f-9abae3dadd4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5188a6-60a0-5e38-9164-201c3eb60cd5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425788}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"} :step/id #uuid "d572a9fc-16a3-5783-b7e1-a3d5a6eab501" :step/math-academy-id 157028}] :activity/title "Disjoint Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152373}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930} :activity/id #uuid "c358bde0-c269-53bb-bce4-8d51f4516e17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185317 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." :tutorial/id #uuid "488db0ee-92f8-5790-8ff6-2977ab0e6dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3187 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "708bbadc-ca98-5148-98dd-f1cc4b9125d0" :step/math-academy-id 653101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184065 :question/id #uuid "2cb88713-f830-501e-9b0a-60320936ad14" :question/math-academy-id "e-4792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485510}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"} :step/id #uuid "0564ce26-4a8b-562e-9ab6-4940e223854b" :step/math-academy-id 653106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184094 :question/id #uuid "132efc65-5f50-5582-8955-a5d34c57af04" :question/math-academy-id "e-8224"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442959}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "ca7e22db-059b-547f-ad06-b3fe2c740ff4" :step/math-academy-id 653103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185345 :tutorial/content "There are many different vector spaces. Some of the most important are listed below.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$, the set of all column vectors of height $n$ with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_n(\\mathbb{R})$, the set of all $n \\times n$ matrices with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the set of all polynomials in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the set of all polynomials of degree less than or equal to $n$ in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The set of all real-valued functions of one variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb7bd53e-69ec-5e14-b970-1bd6ff9aff7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6199 :tutorial/title "Important Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "2b2df015-d32e-5a46-ab00-33e8ccdd4686" :step/math-academy-id 653102 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184420}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008} :activity/id #uuid "71036dcd-e882-527a-9681-dd5cc25371dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54174a8e-4b9f-5398-9c3e-042309cf5af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55b42e-8e25-5d4a-9c6c-fb23853f6637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419001} {:answer/id #uuid "923e4cd4-7cb7-513f-a013-6b459182a21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419002} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a112ad-76a7-5358-b6fe-7be7dc9072ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419003} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa960c-9666-5ef7-ae8f-8608f1b827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} :answer-field/id #uuid "90535267-04a3-5209-88fc-9a5564318779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19147c4b-722c-5ed5-9d91-8a4847b759ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-35626" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f729f32-9ccf-5071-9aab-348325a597e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313cdc82-5d69-5af4-95bb-ace914a012ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43eb35-6698-556b-937a-7540b2eaa194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418997} {:answer/id #uuid "54d7d70f-b06b-5dba-8920-ce4be2335648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418999} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff392f-4de3-5c34-9123-789573d772b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419000} {:answer/id #uuid "f187a6fa-78fb-565f-9a52-c1915abb65f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions  in $A$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628fceb-08d9-5ba5-86ad-00d444e18706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a47334c-0a05-5b9c-b7eb-76cf4c1d5cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94792" :question/problem "Let $A$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form  $f(x)=a+{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because **multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)={x}^{2},$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin A$ because the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in A.$ $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "4418ef90-81d3-58b0-a3d7-e0c226069037"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1337dd4f-9280-53a2-a0bf-10fa5a1172ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385326} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b06213-6b70-5fb1-9074-bd29c49e8d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385327} {:answer/id #uuid "175edfb7-a947-56df-a366-83319bf965d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385328} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ae0dc-f023-5eac-8d6f-6539fcd8b68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385329} {:answer/id #uuid "f383b979-fa8e-5c2b-8977-e24abfd545ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385328} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de75fa4-40ed-54ed-9a38-aaf2ba31ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7e8b0d2-1b58-5e69-8702-c53196883e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-35627" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8de