hen $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "f97b0645-0a69-5367-a434-1456880e7389" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423674}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423657}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485622} :activity/id #uuid "d507fccf-b7e1-5cc3-b520-c32805d9f383" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c610fab-a6ec-5612-9143-78410e0c0685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $T$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423660} {:answer/id #uuid "952805ec-db79-5003-ab44-c608da27648b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "there exists a matrix $A\\in T$ such that $A+B\\ne O$ for any $B\\in T.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423662} {:answer/id #uuid "97a1f3dc-dc3c-53f9-b457-30bca1c9f80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T$ does not contain the zero matrix $O$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423663} {:answer/id #uuid "d008fb5f-392d-5a1e-8353-f48e8e0933c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $T$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423665} {:answer/id #uuid "f89006ab-36a7-5769-850c-8ee5d58a71d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423667} :answer-field/id #uuid "047ee837-df71-5e5c-bcee-335a715a816d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87c89c45-3edb-5cd5-88f1-a3ee2b4d8003" :question/math-academy-id "q-35640" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices $({a}_{ij})$  with real entries, such that  ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}$ is rational.  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because **multiplication by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\n For example, the identity matrix \n\n$$\nI=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nlies in $T$, but\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cdot I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\notin T\n$$\n\nbecause $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2}$ is not a rational number.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $T.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is commutative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in T,$  the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in  $T,$ and   $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "01573a3c-c095-5815-9e2c-e98160adf429" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313cdc82-5d69-5af4-95bb-ace914a012ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43eb35-6698-556b-937a-7540b2eaa194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418997} {:answer/id #uuid "54d7d70f-b06b-5dba-8920-ce4be2335648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418999} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff392f-4de3-5c34-9123-789573d772b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419000} {:answer/id #uuid "f187a6fa-78fb-565f-9a52-c1915abb65f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions  in $A$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628fceb-08d9-5ba5-86ad-00d444e18706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a47334c-0a05-5b9c-b7eb-76cf4c1d5cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94792" :question/problem "Let $A$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form  $f(x)=a+{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because **multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)={x}^{2},$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin A$ because the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in A.$ $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "12714b70-150c-5e2d-9693-0688dd834525" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667d5399-0313-59af-b2e0-802df62fe40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485040} {:answer/id #uuid "72a2d8f5-2bbf-5e10-a196-ab974c840f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485063} {:answer/id #uuid "cb268be9-b031-56df-a9d8-c22c7df876bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485251} {:answer/id #uuid "db62e16b-6046-5b50-b4ef-540d0fefbf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485278} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a695bc-1cc3-56a9-b517-f1c7b9def310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485349} :answer-field/id #uuid "897bc76a-4fe0-590f-a184-6c52a1eda1ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2e41850a-28e4-5827-a5c3-9f3e4e6f3d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-106939" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+w=v+x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37dee290-ae86-5ea7-bbc6-9de005b9ccaf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54174a8e-4b9f-5398-9c3e-042309cf5af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55b42e-8e25-5d4a-9c6c-fb23853f6637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419001} {:answer/id #uuid "923e4cd4-7cb7-513f-a013-6b459182a21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419002} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a112ad-76a7-5358-b6fe-7be7dc9072ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419003} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa960c-9666-5ef7-ae8f-8608f1b827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} :answer-field/id #uuid "90535267-04a3-5209-88fc-9a5564318779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19147c4b-722c-5ed5-9d91-8a4847b759ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-35626" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a435581-1964-5e18-aaa9-6b2d8b1b3a49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148e1662-35f6-5380-bc45-570bd123aa65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of matrices do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357930} {:answer/id #uuid "320b5d11-4a8f-55fe-bba8-195d9e922e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358197} {:answer/id #uuid "48109943-1239-5a7c-af98-06bb37c313df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358370} {:answer/id #uuid "7d249c29-f44d-5240-8308-3b4e8b179337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358839} {:answer/id #uuid "db7e8f9f-3fef-596d-bd51-f3eb1ae8d92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot A\\ne A$ for all $A\\in K$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358370} :answer-field/id #uuid "115fee41-7a55-5085-8c7d-272bb27890fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec097acf-f56e-507d-aaff-ec7126bba2f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94807" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all $2\\times 1$ matrices $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},$ such that $x,y\\in R$ and $x+y=1.$  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because **the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K.$** *  \n\nIndeed, adding the entries of the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix yields $0+0=0\\ne 1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- addition of matrices is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- addition and scalar multiplication of matrices  satisfy the distributive laws. $✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot A=A$ for all $A\\in K.$ $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "7c6bc73d-ee47-53b9-9204-a829025e6e21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485614}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484812}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193681} :activity/id #uuid "b28c80a9-d473-57b6-bbf1-5ee7b422145c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049217 :topic/id #uuid "e446a53c-cc70-58f0-b872-8d6e7a9c434e" :topic/math-academy-id 4123} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192389 :question/id #uuid "2393d607-0f73-59f3-9a5f-ebe45050dda9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048615}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534070}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f15a8c24-10c0-50c8-9701-f1e1122791bf" :step/math-academy-id 38121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193897 :tutorial/content "Likelihood functions of samples drawn from populations modeled using continuous random variables are defined similarly to discrete variables. The only difference is that we use probability density functions instead of probability mass functions.\n\nIf $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed *continuous* random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be continuous, I.I.D. random variables with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nHere, $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Suppose we conduct our random sample and get the following results:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2.5, \\qquad x_2 = 3.2, \\qquad x_3 = 1.2.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, we compute the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =(\\theta {x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot (\\theta {x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯(\\theta {x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot ({x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯({x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}{({x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}⋯{x}_{n})}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have $n=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.5\\cdot 3.2\\cdot 1.2=9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{3} ( 9.6 )^{-\\theta-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e339526-e303-5047-8def-188a04dca982" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a3131cc-aeb5-5d42-af4a-921157386704" :step/math-academy-id 42268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192521 :question/id #uuid "2ff20920-30c5-5027-b461-31b13d2031e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533973}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "9623ba3e-7523-5921-af5c-689ea499d4b4" :step/math-academy-id 42283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192523 :question/id #uuid "bd63ff93-762d-5d7d-be8d-9701130b9fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533983}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bc09f530-58a5-566a-a408-bd7b51bd761e" :step/math-academy-id 42301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193902 :tutorial/content "The probability density function of a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSome example normal distribution curves are shown below. \n\n![](images/c9/c9359fc84401aabb19e526755b70647cb0496fa8ddba65974466e0ffc3dbbbdc.png)\n\nIf we set $\\sigma=1,$ then $f(x)$ simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a standard normal random variable whose mean is shifted right by $\\mu$ units.\n\nFinally, setting the unknown parameter as $\\theta$ when discussing likelihood functions is common. Following this convention, we set $\\mu=\\theta,$ and we get\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's use this to compute some likelihood functions of some normally distributed sample data." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e08170d-c55e-531f-a8b9-99dfa8ecbac7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7019 :tutorial/title "The Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "5912947a-0cd6-5e5a-958c-a4fff1dab140" :step/math-academy-id 42321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193505}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192932}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510483} :activity/id #uuid "7eb5815e-ec1b-5309-9ada-af8c6f2e1f20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049217 :topic/id #uuid "e446a53c-cc70-58f0-b872-8d6e7a9c434e" :topic/math-academy-id 4123} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2332428c-0956-5edc-8fe6-022460583d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509763} {:answer/id #uuid "4099daa4-c8e4-54d9-9751-3571d0836455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509825} {:answer/id #uuid "5e998d67-7741-5d32-adff-5aad49d3a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509884} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdaf55-77a9-5b17-80ef-8da0aeac0d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4.5}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509963} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d4114d-abec-5620-b7d3-44264b2a1f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-5}\\,{e}^{4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509763} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa1de34-af75-5f8e-b326-c843f5d31eb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a460e59-a60e-5c01-b0d6-ac09a000a8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159874" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=4.5.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=4.5$ and $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "123ec72f-798d-521a-b71f-a350724a6d80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a5d853-1f1e-50a0-8e00-2fa1064e40e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{3}(28.7)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509721} {:answer/id #uuid "2146d1c2-145c-5e19-be13-24bf25afba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4}(28.7)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509754} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34928-e7d0-5f70-87a4-51288f7f7d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{28.7}(3)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510074} {:answer/id #uuid "def5dc94-633d-576b-941d-07e8b9c94d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{2}(28.7)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510162} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3b892-df42-5754-afb3-26d55a0f358f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta (28.7)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509721} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a24af9-2c20-5bcb-882d-da71398e4661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67f027bd-5627-5870-ad62-8c955e4cf931" :question/math-academy-id "q-159919" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $4.1,\\,2.5,\\,2.8.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4.1\\cdot 2.5\\cdot 2.8=28.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{3}(28.7)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "581d75e5-f869-52ac-9527-20195bb5d14d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f431101-4c6d-5bf2-ba99-85b859abf503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-8.8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509854} {:answer/id #uuid "534b0bea-259c-590f-b217-8d6213f5b072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{8.8}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509861} {:answer/id #uuid "7d59090d-226b-567f-b107-8cc219edf98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-11}\\,{e}^{8.8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509959} {:answer/id #uuid "7f080b35-a5ae-5e2b-bd64-31274fadcc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509965} {:answer/id #uuid "cb96d045-9d55-5deb-9924-204c6531a1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-8.8}\\,{e}^{11\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509854} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f28c710-ad63-5f90-ab4c-dca1c9762afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7231eacb-7b8d-5da7-a852-73cf2a0b08b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159875" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{11},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{x}_{i}=8.8.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=11$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8.8$ and $n=11.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-8.8\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "758ec95c-458e-58ad-b910-88dba5d83555" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c1d491-402e-555e-a666-7f7c4e87d6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.225-0.35\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509710} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7b057-e13c-59a5-9c17-b9e7479f7400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.45+0.7\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509944} {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} {:answer/id #uuid "8f42f131-75fe-5641-ab8b-d247827641d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.225+0.35\\theta +2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509996} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1fb752-a169-5228-a97e-05992483d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.45-0.7\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8fd110-202a-5667-bf26-f7d318d06cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67f89f72-de60-5983-9ba7-f68316fa9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-159972" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $0.3,\\,1.2,\\,-2.6,\\,0.4$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9adaeb98-58da-5087-9e4e-fb6fed2b0b66"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee6b5b6-79d8-5f3b-a3b5-ea4295831ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-29.4+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509788} {:answer/id #uuid "36bfab97-2213-5db0-bc57-64be646f86f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-29.4+4.49\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509806} {:answer/id #uuid "f0783fcb-15c0-52e8-a012-1b2d6692109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14.7+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510206} {:answer/id #uuid "f801fc62-3fdd-5360-808c-c7be45810737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.98+14.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510216} {:answer/id #uuid "faf6b123-23f4-5859-a6a3-7c67537a6a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.49+29.4\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510206} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9645430-c3dd-5a0d-a5c2-2b90bb8a3076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a232b81-c933-5282-aa6a-6ac51618b1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159951" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i}=8.98,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i}^{2}=29.4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8.98,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=29.4,$ and $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(29.4-2(8.98)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-14.7\\,+\\,8.98\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-14.7+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ae0bb4af-619e-5c31-bd50-96b13e3c5a74" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510526}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509685}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534115} :activity/id #uuid "960b13a3-c1c3-5ef9-a169-b4383d62d63a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049217 :topic/id #uuid "e446a53c-cc70-58f0-b872-8d6e7a9c434e" :topic/math-academy-id 4123} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5f896b-07c8-575f-a95f-543f0a5bfd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-4}\\,{e}^{12.7\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533160} {:answer/id #uuid "41fceb7b-f3b0-57fa-9d14-6589b1b04b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{15.2}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533295} {:answer/id #uuid "b2efa9d6-1c48-546b-89e7-7b6e0f2cd3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{12.7}\\,{e}^{-4\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533571} {:answer/id #uuid "cce52c47-ba66-5b14-afe7-7a95bdded069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-15.2\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533635} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd538ea-0060-5f81-80f2-b4e4bdaf8f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-12.7\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533758} :answer-field/id #uuid "5132bca4-efa9-5309-812c-4a188d9b1def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cee2f557-b129-5694-8dac-fc10e355c8aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-159781" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1,\\,5.0,\\,4.2,\\,1.4.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2.1+5.0+4.2+1.4 \\\\  & =12.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-12.7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c06c6d0-6608-5602-a4f7-50a6f1c7f705" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171f3f1-b42e-5b23-bf4a-d4abdcfda312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.92+12\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533260} {:answer/id #uuid "36743fa8-d11f-5277-bb11-6800921de937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10.04+6\\theta -{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533267} {:answer/id #uuid "44588364-77e7-5293-9871-f77f9b19a264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533298} {:answer/id #uuid "63fa5ce6-de9e-5d28-b5ef-e762a11c1eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.88+12\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533368} {:answer/id #uuid "a04280ae-ca06-57f0-95b1-5cb9b2387e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.12+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533298} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f209dd-c051-5b5e-8018-eed49751c9a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf2980b5-8d36-5df9-841e-821c9785b285" :question/math-academy-id "q-159956" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5},{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $1.7,\\,2.3,\\,2.2,\\,1.1,\\,1.9,\\,1.8$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.7+2.3+2.2+1.1+1.9+1.8 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.7)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(2.2)^{2}+(1.1)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(1.8)^{2} \\\\  & =21.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(21.08-2(11)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-10.54\\,+\\,11\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4e350e27-f534-5973-89a1-9b9023c54815"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c10a342-33a3-5012-8a4e-3198139f6867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533321} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4ba814-6782-55d8-96db-e50161653b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22.6+5.2\\theta -6{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533347} {:answer/id #uuid "b40ee415-1e01-58ec-a195-7fc6ee04552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.3-2.6\\theta +3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533577} {:answer/id #uuid "c5536e13-2ef3-5e73-85a5-44744385cc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11.3+2.6\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533613} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9e2870-85b7-5a1c-951e-e909b21fa61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.3-5.2\\theta +3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533321} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4b3a32-9b08-55e4-95ee-7361270b62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0731a97b-5085-5ebd-87e8-36cd1dc34ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159952" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{6},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}=5.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e476eca8-0ffe-50fa-a237-82906860cdaa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cdbb19-c3e1-50e9-95b6-dba7968abaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-6.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533144} {:answer/id #uuid "09db5eb7-a0d5-5003-ad1f-55526d2c8fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-5}\\,{e}^{7.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533157} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2a5e69-fccf-5474-9c74-f9ae4d8263eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-6.5}\\,{e}^{5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533325} {:answer/id #uuid "c55c269b-4229-5d74-ac99-7a306e45927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{7.5}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533614} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9077c-1504-5150-89a1-24c8262596a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533719} :answer-field/id #uuid "66bb6848-6df8-5544-91de-3216e134df6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0422034f-fdb1-53c7-b35d-c57baa06d2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159886" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.6,\\,1.8,\\,0.7,\\,2.9,\\,1.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e83b57d7-6f13-51b1-83b6-66e62682df33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0681314e-d960-5bb5-a4e3-5fe913bb01ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.2-16.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533150} {:answer/id #uuid "0f708f9a-916c-5539-8d5f-bfdabc63a213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13.2-16.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533177} {:answer/id #uuid "790804e9-daa4-5fb8-bde3-59169ded9144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33.2+13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533426} {:answer/id #uuid "dc99796b-4535-56e1-be58-6cc8406c3823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533682} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb41dee-3cc0-53a3-9343-0f5c275f0862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16.6+13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533682} :answer-field/id #uuid "b697133a-5977-5733-b1c6-65040dd5324d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b81cde63-dfd8-59f4-90cc-c6262f4bcfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-159954" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=-13.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-13.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-16/2}\\,{e}^{-(33.2-2(-13.2)\\theta +16{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,-16.6\\,-\\,13.2\\theta \\,-\\,8{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fbd0bbfd-7919-581a-90bb-f17f8b597f32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534122}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533126}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124} :activity/id #uuid "ffe0a9ff-b89d-5315-a9ba-afafd34b3fa7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153391 :tutorial/content "We know how to define the double integral of a function over a rectangular domain. Now, we'll learn how to define a double integral over a non-rectangular region.\n\nLet's consider the non-rectangular region $D$ enclosed by the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown below. \n\n![](images/e8/e80bcbaff16282f00e7e3df487d78cfbc51bb2e9660dde5e2fc421cd4d70a9af.png)\n\n \n\nWe will define the following double integral over this non-rectangular region:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nTo do this, we will use the following trick. Let's enclose the region $D$ by a rectangle $R$ with sides parallel to the coordinates axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/21/214db95d949a8658e3fdbfea8f54c0e3bf914c11d9ef88e1ca4264d64d360c90.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we extend our function to $R$ by setting a new function $f(x,y)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $D$ and equals $3-x^2-2y^2$ on $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab8eed93d71415d810f4c8c9b19d690317769e270805a6925d35f5f0e4c9671f.svg)\n\n![](images/43/43288ccc496832a1a78f9fcaaabb4a5841fc79db5d2082dff7d4177005b61d01.png)\n\n \n\nAlthough $f(x,y)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $D,$ the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over the rectangular domain $R$ will exist. \n\nTherefore, we define our double integral of $3-x^2-2y^2$ over $D$ as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the value of the double integral will give us the volume of the solid bounded by the surface $z=3-x^2-2y^2$ over the quarter-circular domain $D.$\n\n**Watch out!** The domain $R$ must not necessarily be the smallest possible rectangle that covers the region $D.$ It can be any rectangular domain that covers $D$ completely." :tutorial/id #uuid "14a20ee8-f248-507f-bce6-8dc6be91b9e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5df5591-1e92-560d-ba7c-bbf286cf8b6b" :step/math-academy-id 25787 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152005 :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482597}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9b86ebc6-eb40-565f-a521-f246f439bdd2" :step/math-academy-id 25788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534051}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "a01eeffe-e0b1-5e2a-be46-b8854fed7312" :step/math-academy-id 25790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152007 :question/id #uuid "fe8eb478-6830-55bc-84c6-668924daaefc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6997"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436043}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "3132ab29-3ec9-50e2-aca1-43d58e9447c0" :step/math-academy-id 25792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153125}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152328}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407300} :activity/id #uuid "b8cf7360-113f-5967-a53f-923c8be1dfcf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567abe3c-003e-5470-895b-6e4de6668d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460281} {:answer/id #uuid "42a6b8b5-f337-585c-81c8-58ef0adc78c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460281} :answer-field/id #uuid "539f1f0a-a5c7-5cf1-8dfe-991c5ed6a3c9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229a4b0e-7cc8-578e-9c98-62c05f0575cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460283} {:answer/id #uuid "420109c7-1b02-5fdd-bd56-6921fde84d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460283} :answer-field/id #uuid "759a1a61-0534-5515-a7d7-778c990a26c4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461008} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a98d4383-44c4-517a-8eb0-cc5ae603ebcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460285} {:answer/id #uuid "0c907cc0-6af1-5efa-95fa-10890d40d058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460286} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e48dc1a-6694-5774-95fd-e6de685299f0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8098b383-f4a9-5d0e-9352-b359bad47678" :question/math-academy-id "q-80430" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "080248f8-ed70-5a9f-8bf1-a2b4de4eb4f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d87facea-76c9-53bf-b25e-e7acd8a0848f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472616} {:answer/id #uuid "34788671-40c0-55de-9282-705047e61c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472617} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cca8d7-a733-550e-ab33-1ebcc8cc6ba3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb2a430-1b20-5cd8-889c-8397d5f66f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472618} {:answer/id #uuid "facde33f-6a68-5c26-a7d0-f157550ab480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472619} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b1f945-9ba0-5e99-9ab4-a0c22108dd3f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4df998-df9d-5be1-85e2-6c7fe20e4d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472620} {:answer/id #uuid "94aa333b-da6a-5843-999a-18b8e9d9555f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472620} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b668eb0-a01c-5c4c-9775-6cc00549cbbd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04b43074-9710-54c1-a656-7d7797008d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-129786" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d66cb97b9e04367ce2250e5156eaa9daaf42fadb395220e0464bca687e91908.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$\n- ${∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "30bd25e3-ac80-5e5b-b4e0-5224a24a94ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e019259c-a480-51c7-adee-7e64d30db325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460275} {:answer/id #uuid "3c62bbae-8481-50c2-900d-f50750addcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460275} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed25a22e-c812-5c51-97bf-3f552b015487" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f843948-205f-56d3-9549-f072aa94f578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460277} {:answer/id #uuid "b8af677d-062c-5280-a8a3-61adf659040c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460278} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f5cff7-ebff-5f64-bebb-f05d813e5cf0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0c58ef-21d5-557b-b1f3-8d8357abcf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460279} {:answer/id #uuid "31b0c3fa-e8ff-5c10-b771-f69f3886b0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460279} :answer-field/id #uuid "43970d5a-70f7-5eaf-9c95-866b3155b96e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ca2c9d4-83c1-5596-b062-1537df902124" :question/math-academy-id "q-80425" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d09a23a1ae9f737c7f19ba83d6d20c3fbcea3c7deb71fa307264c90afabc6580.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/16/1620acdcfd3783df478322ebe5e81cb274d908db12d9c8bdc701fe21070b828f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4e94e485-56a2-5844-806a-2096ce99769f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d785f01e-c01b-508b-a7e4-0b68e3819afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473512} {:answer/id #uuid "72475eb4-0b35-5d4b-9ea7-4c75ae1d9bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473513} :answer-field/id #uuid "7063df18-38c8-5001-85dc-c37c2a884180" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474151} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45915786-bde2-58ed-b09f-01d02dd4ab1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473514} {:answer/id #uuid "db2767a5-0290-566a-8860-446c19d11819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473514} :answer-field/id #uuid "9428d01a-a858-5759-ad6e-dec6825a2e03" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20decd90-c71f-5b9c-af2d-3a173c83d963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473516} {:answer/id #uuid "025119c3-228a-5031-89d8-d102fe48b0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473516} :answer-field/id #uuid "31799862-7294-50e6-9e5a-cbd879317060" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed965e5b-f0df-5c0f-9c7c-0cb7a433c067" :question/math-academy-id "q-86866" :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "5f132e51-d1d4-5811-8af1-98e3b3699b32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d7503-b6f8-527c-a973-ac2bfc867df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472622} {:answer/id #uuid "77d96fda-e870-55c8-b23f-4d1d5eb0e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623} :answer-field/id #uuid "256d9380-5a22-5d58-bc62-7da50bb3ece8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f3b95a-f8ca-5464-96e2-8800e62a2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472624} {:answer/id #uuid "50306918-6a2a-5e3c-a5ab-150d520b00cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472624} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d53d61-4f6d-56eb-96a8-a48708fcd15a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642ff713-a98e-574b-ab8d-f4ec58c4a354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472626} {:an